#!/bin/bash
# backup-box/help.loc - Module-specific help

cat <<'EOF'
Module: backup-box - offsite backup box holding ciphertext only

  Provisions a box that lives on someone else's network and stores encrypted
  restic repositories it has no key for and cannot read.

  RETURNING COLD? Read backup-box/README.md first - it is the design document
  for the whole backup system, not just this module. Short version:

    - The SOURCE encrypts before anything leaves the LAN. The repository
      password never exists on this box, so a stolen or rooted box reveals
      nothing: no contents, no filenames, no directory structure.
    - The box cannot reach the LAN. It gets a /32 on both ends of the tunnel
      and the hub drops every connection it originates. The source always
      initiates, so that policy has zero exceptions.
    - There is NO full-disk encryption, deliberately. It would protect only
      revocable credentials, at the cost of a box that cannot reboot
      unattended. See README.md.
    - rest-server runs --append-only, so a compromised box cannot destroy
      backup history.

SUBCOMMANDS

  storage                        Claim unallocated volume-group space for the
                                 backup volume. Reports first; acts on -e yes=1.
      yamc -h HOST backup-box storage
      yamc -h HOST backup-box storage -e yes=1
      yamc -h HOST backup-box storage -e bk_vg=ubuntu-vg -e bk_size=95%FREE

  setup                          Install rest-server, create repositories and
                                 credentials. Prints new passwords ONCE.
      yamc -h HOST backup-box -e bk_users=uranus
      yamc -h HOST backup-box -e bk_users="uranus sun" -e yes=1

  lockdown                       Egress policy. RUN THIS LAST - it is the step
                                 that can lock you out. Auto-reverts after 60s
                                 unless -e permanent=1.
      yamc -h HOST backup-box lockdown
      yamc -h HOST backup-box lockdown -e yes=1
      yamc -h HOST backup-box lockdown -e yes=1 -e permanent=1

  status                         Tunnel, storage, service, repositories, rules.
      yamc -h HOST backup-box status

CONFIRMATION MODEL

  Anything destructive or lockout-prone prints its full plan, then:

    -e yes=1 given      proceed without asking
    terminal available  prompt
    no terminal         refuse (yamcity redirects stdin from /dev/null, so a
                        prompt there would read EOF and silently proceed)

  The plan is printed by the same code path that does the work, so a report
  cannot drift out of sync with the action.

PARAMETERS

  bk_mount    /srv/backup     mount point for the repository volume
  bk_vg       autodetected    volume group (refuses if ambiguous)
  bk_lv       backup          logical volume name
  bk_size     100%FREE        lvcreate -l argument
  bk_fstype   ext4            filesystem (ext4 gets -m 0: no root reserve)
  bk_iface    bk0             tunnel interface
  bk_addr     from bk_iface   bind address for rest-server
  bk_port     8000            rest-server port
  bk_users    (required)      rest-server users, one per source host

DEPLOYMENT ORDER

  Full procedure in README.md. The parts people skip and regret:

    - SEED ON SITE, at LAN speed. Terabytes over a residential uplink is not
      a first run, it is a month.
    - TUNNEL TEST FROM A FOREIGN NETWORK before the box leaves. Tether it to
      a phone hotspot and confirm an unattended tunnel and a real backup.
      Otherwise the first genuine test of dial-out happens somewhere you
      cannot reach.
    - Run 'lockdown' LAST, after the tunnel is proven from the final site.
    - KEEP THE EXISTING RSYNC MIRROR until you have restored something real.

SAFETY

  'storage' is the only part that can destroy data, and is deliberately timid:

    - reports by default, acts only on explicit confirmation
    - refuses if the logical volume already exists - never reuses, never
      reformats
    - mkfs only ever runs on an LV created in the same invocation, and only
      after blkid confirms it is blank
    - never extends, resizes or touches the root LV
    - refuses and reports on anything unexpected rather than getting clever

VERIFICATION IS NOT OPTIONAL

  An encrypted repository is opaque: a silently failed backup looks exactly
  like a working one. Freshness alarms and 'restic check' belong on the
  SOURCE host - a compromised box is precisely the one that will report
  itself healthy. 'backup-box status' is an operator convenience, not a
  health check.

SEE ALSO

  wg-hub    hub side of the tunnel
  wg-peer   spoke side of the tunnel
EOF
